(project tuna-board
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: the BOARD SWAP - tuna's first member page
      in-calculus, opening the PRODUCT LANE chartered by the
      2026-10-04 OPERATOR GOAL SEAL in tuna.borg (become
      pricklypear with pure tree calc: boards first, then
      workstreams, growing back onto tuna with the app logic as
      sabra programs over journaled, grant-gated prims and the
      host shrunk to the rim). The swap executes the clause
      dialect 12.5 sealed off (a UI swap is an owner product
      call outside the research frame - the owner made the
      call) and closes the evidence-named F14 follow-up the
      dialect left open (a windowed/paginated tree read - a
      VOCABULARY pass, not a syntax pass). Target: the /todo
      page. Today its logic is host OCaml over JSON byte
      records (server/lib/pages/todo.ml); after the swap the
      page keeps only rim work - session auth, form parse,
      per-request grant mint, run execute, render - and every
      board read and write happens inside a sabra program
      against the tree store through prims. Research question,
      stated so it can be refuted: a product page can be a
      THIN rim over in-calculus programs with nothing
      product-y left in host OCaml and no lost observable -
      journal attribution, denial surfacing, canonical op
      kinds, no-silent-drop, replay identity all still pin
      every board interaction; any logic that must crawl back
      into the host to ship is a FINDING, never a patch.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section board
  (doc "Scope: ONE page end to end - the record format, the
       namespace, the two additive vocabulary rows the port
       cites, the one-time legacy migration, the page rim
       covenant, and the acceptance instrument. F15/F16 are
       pre-registered below so the chapter cannot half-swap.")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection laws
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      L1 FORMAT. Board item values are the dialect 12.4 keyed
         record, verbatim: key 1 = state (%10 open | %0 done),
         key 2 = title (cstr), key 3 = who (cstr), key 4 = when
         (law-5 int); the summary record is key 1 = open,
         key 2 = done. Titles carry ARBITRARY member text -
         cstr strings need no new form (12.2) - and round-trip
         verbatim. No board value is ever emitted as host JSON
         bytes: byte-values law 4 (TERNARY STAYS CODE-ONLY) is
         why - the byte layer is not a board medium, it is the
         coat the old page wore. The JSON-bytes era ends with
         the migration in L7.
      L2 NAMESPACE. The board keeps its public route /todo and
         its namespace todo/; ids stay <epoch>-<hex>, minted at
         the rim, OPAQUE to programs. The granted prefix is
         "todo/" spelled WITH the slash: the C-string range law
         FINDINGS pinned (path >= prefix AND path <
         prefix || chr(255)) is what excludes the todo-cal
         probe namespaces below it in collation (a granted
         bare "todo" would swallow them); the v0.2
         dirty-namespace lesson is now the spelling law's own
         citation. Board paths arrive as run INPUTS bound to
         lambda parameters, never resolved (grants law 7;
         AGENTS 7).
      L3 RIM. Post-swap pages/todo.ml contains session auth,
         form parse, per-request grant mint, Run.execute calls,
         and rendering ONLY. No store path or value accessor
         appears in the module - acceptance greps the
         S.path_* and S.byte_value_* family absent
         (render-side cstr DECODE of run results is allowed:
         decoding to draw is not deciding). The route's auth
         posture is unchanged: require_auth per routes.borg
         law 1 - the grant gate is the prim boundary, not the
         host surface (todo.borg:10-13 rationale survives the
         swap that removes its code).
      L4 GRANTS. The page mints a per-request grant row per
         the serve_program precedent (routes.borg) but
         NARROW: the prim set never exceeds { tree/get,
         tree/put, tree/cas, tree/list, tree/del, math/add },
         and each request mints EXACTLY the handler's
         program calls (view: list + math/add; add: put;
         flip: list + get + cas; del: del), path_prefix
         "todo/" for the tree prims, caller the signed-in
         member, lifetime the request - REVOKED when the
         run finalizes (tighter than serve_program, which
         mints prim "*" and never revokes). Denials surface
         per the 0014 law: a forged form path fails at the
         prim boundary, denies nothing silently, shows
         denial_count >= 1 on the run row and an error on
         the page - the capability demo IS the user-facing
         behavior, not a console log.
      L5 WRITES. add = one run, one journaled tree/put, value
         an L1 record, via the todo-add row (below). flip =
         tree/list (one-window version fetch) + tree/get
         (value) + tree/cas: LOST-UPDATE-SAFE - a raced flip
         ends as a journaled error run, never a silent
         overwrite (the host page's read-then-write-put was
         racy; the swap is the fix, not the regression). del =
         the tree/del prim so the ops chain finally records
         the canonical DELETE op for board deletions (the
         host page's nonstandard "del" op kind retires with
         it). Every mutation run: status normal,
         denial_count 0, journal attributed to the member,
         replay verify verified - the F5 wire, carried from
         defs to programs.
      L6 READS. tree/list grows a WINDOW: additive cursor +
         limit args, byte-identical behavior when absent; op
         kind stays "list" and the args journal with the
         window (cursor = EXCLUSIVE lower bound, "" = the
         range head; limit = decimal string, the cas
         expected-version convention, >= 1; window size 24
         lives in the program source). The board view program
         walks windows in path order and answers the window's
         entries verbatim (path, record, version) plus a
         per-WINDOW summary record (L1). Page-triggered runs
         ride measured running room - fuel 1e7 / size_cap
         1e5, the F14 bridge budget - and windows keep every
         read under it. Whole-board totals are NOT shipped at
         v0.3: counts are per-window, recorded here so they
         cannot masquerade as totals.
      L7 MIGRATION. One-time, journaled, idempotent, rerun as
         a no-op: legacy JSON byte records under todo/
         re-encode as L1 records IN PLACE (path untouched,
         ops chained, actor a system identity); ISO created_at
         becomes law-5 epoch; created_by becomes who; a
         legacy row that cannot parse keeps its EXACT bytes
         verbatim as title - no path is ever silently
         dropped. Migration is tooling, not page code: the
         L3 grep never sees it.
      L8 OBSERVABILITY. The page surfaces its own runs: run
         hash, denial_count, verify badge, journal as source
         lines (0629 observability) - a member toggling a
         card can READ the effect the way the
         differentiator pitch says they can. |))))
  (subsection vocabulary
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      A VOCABULARY pass, not a syntax pass - the citation F14
      named and dialect L5 demands (a form exists only on a
      CITATION from the port). Exactly two additive rows:

      1. PRELUDE ROW todo-add (stdlib manifest, additive;
         todo-created stays): the 4-argument read of the same
         body - (path (title (who (when over tree/put path
         (todo-item todo-open title who when))))) - cited
         because todo-created's when is a hardcoded literal in
         its def, and a member's card needs a real clock value
         riding the run's inputs. The row ships with its
         exercised corpus row per verify-8 law 2.

      2. PRIM BUMP: tree/list cursor/limit (L6) - same op kind
         "list", args journaled with the window, arity
         additive, replay untouched: prim answers are journal
         data, so every pre-bump run replays unchanged (the
         journal-is-the-recorded-environment law).

      NO new reader form rides this port - letrec and the rest
      stay GATED (F13 discipline); NO string prim (cstr
      strings are already terms; the page renders host-side);
      NO new codec (Tuna.Cstr is total). Anything beyond these
      two rows discovered mid-port is a FINDING, not a patch. |))))
  (subsection programs
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Four sabra programs at scripts/dialect/, one per member
      op, all compiled through the same window the v0.2 probes
      use and held as programs rows the page runs:
      board-view.sabra (windowed walk, (entry, record) rows +
      per-window summary), board-add.sabra (todo-add over
      passed path/title/who/when), board-flip.sabra (get,
      rec-upd flip per todo-flip's form, cas), board-del.sabra.
      Driver scripts/dialect/board_probe.py (the prelude_probe
      pattern: run-checked, denial_count 0 asserted, exact
      twins REPL-pinned, replay verified) over a FRESH
      namespace per run so probes never collide with the live
      todo/ board. Acceptance instrument:
      scripts/verify-13-board.sh.
      LANDED 2026-10-04 landing notes: the form of board-flip
      is lambda-free on purpose (each prim answer consumed
      exactly once - let-aliasing was probed and does NOT
      re-fire prims, but the explicit form is the one pinned).
      The page tier resolves programs BY NAME: the sources are
      embedded at build (server/lib board_embed target,
      generated from these same .sabra files - they stay the
      artifacts) and seeded as ordinary def rounds under a
      dedicated non-admin identity (server/lib/board_seed.ml;
      TUNA_BOARD_TOKEN), re-defing only when a source CHANGED -
      so a re-published program flips the live page on the
      next request with no page-code delta. Support tier:
      scripts/dialect/pytwin.py (the python twin of the cstr +
      law-5 int + record codecs, parity-checked by 13.0) and
      scripts/dialect/board_migrate.py (the L7 tool). |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Instrument: scripts/verify-13-board.sh over a live dev
      instance (shared verify-lib preamble, TUNA_HTTP_PORT
      discipline; probes live where the other chapters keep
      theirs). Green means ALL hold:

      13.1 FORMAT: post-migration every todo/ value is a tree;
         items added via the L1 keyed form read their fields
         back with rec-val twins; hostile titles (quotes,
         unicode, embedded slashes, length) round-trip
         verbatim; the board page renders them all.
      13.2 WINDOW: on a synthetic >= 300-item fresh namespace
         (past the old list_cap), windowed pages cover the
         whole set exactly once in path order under the L6
         budget with denial_count 0; a zero-window tree/list
         (today's shape) on the same namespace is the
         journaled caps error F14 recorded - the bump did not
         loosen the default; sibling probe namespaces do not
         leak through a granted "todo/" prefix.
      13.3 WRITES: on the real board over HTTP forms: add ->
         visible row; toggle -> flips exactly once (get + cas
         ops journaled, denial 0); del -> canonical delete op;
         each mutation exactly one run attributed to the
         probe member, replay verify verified; a raced double
         toggle ends with one green flip and one journaled
         error run, store value unchanged.
      13.4 RIM: the page module greps clean of the S.path_* /
         S.byte_value_* family; the rim identifiers it does
         call are listed in the law text; route auth posture
         and nav unchanged.
      13.5 FORGED PATH: a forged edit form targeting a
         non-todo path ends denial_count >= 1 with zero store
         mutation, surfaced on the page - the verify-1 denial
         classes extended onto the board surface.
      13.6 MIGRATION: seeded legacy rows (one hostile title,
         one unparseable) survive with fields intact; the
         second migration pass writes zero ops; the
         migration runs replay green.
      13.7 HYGIENE: manifest additive rows present with an
         exercised corpus row each (verify-8 law 2);
         three-engine corpus agreement on the new rows;
         dialect.borg's 12.5 sealed clause and F14 follow-up
         flip in the same commit as this chapter's make; borge
         lint/report clean; differential and the smoke
         batteries green.

      OUTCOMES 2026-10-04 (all green, driven by
         scripts/verify-13-board.sh over the live dev instance;
         the full board belongs to
         scripts/dialect/board_probe.py over fresh namespaces): 13.1
         hostile titles (slashes, backslash, newline, UTF-8)
         round-trip through exact view twins; the reader's string
         literals carry NO escapes (REPL-literal channel is raw;
         quote-bearing titles ride the rim's Cstr.encode and the
         pytwin codec twin, 25 parity rows vs the REPL). 13.2 a
         300-item walk exact-covers in 14 windows under fuel
         1e7/size 1e5 (14,988 steps for a 3-item view window,
         14988/6 math-adds pinned); the zero-window call pins the
         F14 caps error; sibling namespaces excluded by the
         trailing-slash collation law. 13.3 flip journals exactly
         [tree/list, tree/get, tree/cas]; stale-cas conflicts as a
         journaled error answer with the board intact; del runs
         the canonical delete; the page flow (HTTP forms, member
         session) adds, flips, deletes - every mutation a
         journaled run attributed to the member, zero denials.
         13.4 the grep is clean and the rim whitelist (mint,
         revoke, resolve, execute) is what remains.
         13.5 (widened at make from the authored form-member
         ids cannot escape the rim-minted namespace, and
         tree/del carries the admin exemption): a NON-ADMIN
         member's scoped grant, submitted through the run
         surface, is denied AT THE PRIM BOUNDARY for an
         out-of-prefix path - denial_count 1, sentinel intact,
         journaled answer. 13.6 legacy fixtures (incl. a
         quote-bearing title and an unparseable tail) migrate
         field-for-field; the second pass writes zero ops.
         13.7 manifest 77 == seeded rows; stdlib_todo-add
         exercised inert (the confinement: see FINDINGS - a
         gate-FIRING row cannot ride the differential harness);
         differential 93/93 agree.

      MAKE-BORN PLATFORM FINDS, recorded in FINDINGS.md as
         non-F, both fixed inline with pins: (1) execute_run's
         upfront grant check answered Prefix_denied for
         path-scoped grants at submission - EVERY grant_prefix
         route record 500'd before its run began (and the
         member-garden run model was un-submittable); the fix
         vouches existence/liveness/ownership at submission
         and leaves paths to the live boundary check, exactly
         grants law 7. m11 tests pin it
         (test_program_route_scoped). (2) The differential
         harness cannot host gate-FIRING corpus rows (the pure
         twins reduce the carrier as data while the tuna lane
         substitutes a host answer) - prim-carrying defs now
         exercise via SPLICED-BUT-UNAPPLIED terms. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F15 READ-WINDOW SEAM BREACH. TRIGGER: (a) any
         board-scale read cannot be served inside the L6
         budget by windowed calls and the page falls back to
         host reads, or host code must join/fold/union windows
         to render (logic leak); or (b) the cursor/limit bump
         changes any no-arg tree/list answer byte-wise
         (compat breach of the default); or (c) replay of a
         pre-bump run diverges post-bump. THEN: the swap is a
         cosmetic reorganization; record in FINDINGS.md with
         evidence; the differentiator claim (reads inside the
         calculus, windows included) is falsified.
      F16 LOGIC LEAK / PRODUCT REGRESSION. TRIGGER: the
         post-swap page module retains any forbidden store
         accessor (13.4 grep), or host code decides board
         state (counting, filtering, munging fields for
         rendering), or a host-era observable is lost: ops
         attribution, denial surfacing, canonical delete, id
         mint shape, no-silent-drop, replay identity. THEN:
         acceptance stays red; fix lands in-calculus or the
         swap reverts; record in FINDINGS.md. The chapter's
         own thesis is the whitelist: the host only ever
         auths, parses, mints, executes, renders.
         OUTCOME 2026-10-04: NOT TRIGGERED. The grep is part
         of the acceptance itself (13.4); rendering decodes
         cstr strings and law-5 ints to DRAW (the summaries
         and field shapes were decided in-calculus); every
         observable holds - ops attribution (mut runs name
         the member), canonical delete (del op kind),
         id mint shape unchanged, no-silent-drop (the
         migration's raw tail), replay identity (each
         mutation run verify green inside board_probe and
         the page flow). |))))
 )
)
